Adaptive Human-Centric Economic System (AHCES)

A Cybernetic Control Architecture for Motivational Stability in Post‑Scarcity Civilizations

Ouadi Maakoul

Sixth Draft — Conservation‑Law‑Compliant Dynamics, Full Lyapunov Derivation, and Institutional Safeguards

---

Abstract

The central challenge of post‑scarcity economics is not the distribution of material abundance but the regulation of human motivation across heterogeneous populations. Empirical evidence from unconditional cash transfer experiments demonstrates that survival support increases well‑being without inducing mass labor withdrawal; yet it also reveals that well‑being gains do not automatically translate into sustained innovation. The Adaptive Human‑Centric Economic System (AHCES) reframes the economy as a closed‑loop cybernetic organism in which motivation—not labor—is the primary controlled variable. This paper presents a fully coupled, conservation‑law‑compliant dynamical model integrating (1) reputation decay to prevent status ossification, (2) time‑dependent atrophy coupling to counteract psychological disengagement, and (3) a proportional‑integral resilience buffer that adjusts unconditional support in response to societal stress. We provide a complete Lyapunov stability derivation for the reduced 2‑dimensional population system and establish global asymptotic stability conditions for the full coupled system (detailed Jacobian construction supplied in Supplementary Material). An operational measurement layer translates theoretical variables into empirically tractable indices, and a multi‑level governance architecture with explicit institutional safeguards—including transparent weight‑setting procedures for the Societal Stability Index and a defined recoverability threshold—ensures legitimacy and accountability. A concrete research roadmap spanning agent‑based simulation, institutional field pilots, and AI‑assisted policy tuning is outlined. AHCES is proposed not as a static welfare model but as a control‑theoretic framework for maintaining motivational homeostasis in civilizations transitioning beyond labor scarcity.

Keywords: Post‑scarcity economics, cybernetic control, motivation regulation, universal basic income, reputation dynamics, Lyapunov stability, Self‑Determination Theory, Ostrom commons

