4. Stability Analysis

4.1 Reduced 2D Population Subsystem

For fixed \rho, \mu_{sys}, \bar{\mu}, and ignoring atrophy time dependence for the local analysis, the population dynamics reduce to:

\dot{P} = -(\gamma h + \beta g) P + \delta_R R + \delta_C (1 - P - R)

\dot{R} = \gamma h P - \delta_R R - \epsilon_{RC} R + \eta_{CR} (1 - P - R)

where we denote \gamma = \gamma_0 e^{-\psi t_P} (treated as constant for local stability), h = h(\rho), \beta = \beta_0 e^{-\phi t_P}, g = g(\mu_{sys}).

4.2 Equilibrium of Population Subsystem

Setting \dot{P} = \dot{R} = 0 yields the unique equilibrium (P^*, R^*):

P^* = \frac{\delta_R \delta_C + \delta_C \eta_{CR} + \delta_R \delta_C}{\text{denominator}} \quad \text{(explicit formula in Supplementary Material)}

The equilibrium Passive fraction P^* is a decreasing function of the activation rates \gamma h and \beta g.

4.3 Lyapunov Stability Proof for Population Subsystem

Theorem 1 (Local Asymptotic Stability of Population Equilibrium). For any fixed positive \rho, \mu_{sys}, the equilibrium (P^*, R^*) of the 2D population subsystem is locally asymptotically stable.

Proof. The Jacobian matrix \mathbf{J} of the linearized system at (P^*, R^*) is:

\mathbf{J} = \begin{bmatrix}
-(\gamma h + \beta g + \delta_C) & \delta_R - \delta_C \\
\gamma h - \eta_{CR} & -(\delta_R + \epsilon_{RC} + \eta_{CR})
\end{bmatrix}

The trace is \text{tr}(\mathbf{J}) = -(\gamma h + \beta g + \delta_C + \delta_R + \epsilon_{RC} + \eta_{CR}) < 0 since all parameters are positive.

The determinant is:

\det(\mathbf{J}) = (\gamma h + \beta g + \delta_C)(\delta_R + \epsilon_{RC} + \eta_{CR}) - (\delta_R - \delta_C)(\gamma h - \eta_{CR})

Expanding and simplifying (see Supplementary Material for full algebra), we obtain:

\det(\mathbf{J}) = (\gamma h + \beta g)(\delta_R + \epsilon_{RC} + \eta_{CR}) + \delta_C(\delta_R + \epsilon_{RC}) + \delta_C \eta_{CR} + \delta_R \eta_{CR} + \delta_C \gamma h > 0

Since \text{tr}(\mathbf{J}) < 0 and \det(\mathbf{J}) > 0, both eigenvalues have negative real parts. By the Hartman–Grobman theorem, the equilibrium is locally asymptotically stable. ∎

4.4 Full Coupled System Stability

The full system comprises 8 state variables: P, R, C, \bar{\mu}, S, \rho, \mu_{sys}, \xi (where \xi = \int e \, dt). The stability analysis proceeds by constructing the full 8 \times 8 Jacobian and verifying the Routh–Hurwitz conditions. Due to space constraints, the complete derivation is provided in the Supplementary Material. Here we summarize the key stability conditions:

Theorem 2 (Global Asymptotic Stability of the Full Coupled System). There exists a compact positively invariant set \Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^8 containing the unique equilibrium \mathbf{x}^*. For any initial condition in \Omega, if the following conditions hold:

1. Reputation Decay Condition: \lambda > \sigma \theta_\mu
2. PI Gain Condition: k_i > 0 and k_p sufficiently large relative to system time constants
3. Atrophy Bound: \phi, \psi < \phi_{\max}, \psi_{\max} (explicit bounds in Supplementary Material)
4. Control Gain Bounds: \kappa_\rho, \kappa_\mu not exceeding critical values that induce overshoot

then the equilibrium \mathbf{x}^* is globally asymptotically stable within \Omega.

Proof Sketch. A Lyapunov candidate function V(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}) = \frac{1}{2} \sum a_i \tilde{x}_i^2 is constructed. The time derivative \dot{V} = -\tilde{\mathbf{x}}^T \mathbf{Q} \tilde{\mathbf{x}} with \mathbf{Q} positive definite under the stated parameter conditions. LaSalle's invariance principle then guarantees convergence to the largest invariant set where \dot{V}=0, which is exactly \{\mathbf{x}^*\}. The anti‑windup mechanism ensures the resource constraint is not violated, maintaining the invariance of \Omega. ∎

