5. Operational Measurement Layer

5.1 Societal Stability Index S(t)

S(t) is a composite index constructed from three sub‑indices, each normalized to [0,1]:

Component Measurement Source Weight
Subjective Well‑Being National well‑being surveys (e.g., OECD Better Life Index, Gallup World Poll) 0.40
Social Trust Generalized trust question (World Values Survey) 0.35
Civic Participation Voter turnout, volunteer rates, community organization membership 0.25

Weight Justification. The weights are derived from principal component analysis of cross‑national well‑being data (Helliwell et al., 2023), where the first principal component explains approximately 60% of variance and loads roughly equally on these three dimensions. Alternative weighting schemes can be employed based on cultural context; the governance mechanism for weight revision is specified in §6.2.

5.2 Innovation Output I(t)

Dimension Indicators Weight
Technological Patents (quality‑adjusted), open‑source contributions, R&D spending 0.40
Economic Total Factor Productivity growth, new firm formation rate 0.35
Cultural Creative works, scientific publications (citation‑weighted) 0.25

5.3 Reputation Level \mu_i and \bar{\mu}(t)

Individual reputation is operationalized through Subjective Validation Pools at the local governance layer:

· Quadratic Funding Rounds: Community members allocate tokens to projects or individuals; the matching formula amplifies broad support.
· Peer Endorsement Graphs: Directed, weighted endorsements with decay applied to stale endorsements.
· Contribution Verification Oracles: Decentralized verification of claimed contributions.

The population average \bar{\mu}(t) is computed as the mean of normalized individual reputation scores within a jurisdiction.

5.4 Behavioral State Estimation P(t), R(t), C(t)

Crucial Design Principle: Classification is performed only on aggregate, anonymized population statistics, never on identifiable individuals.

Estimation methods:

· Labor Force Surveys: Traditional employment/unemployment/participation data.
· Time‑Use Surveys: Detailed diaries of daily activities.
· Machine Learning on Anonymized Data: Classifiers trained on SDT‑validated survey instruments.

Privacy guarantees are implemented via differential privacy: noise is added to all aggregate statistics before use.

5.5 Psychological Atrophy Time t_P

t_P is estimated as the median duration of disengagement within the Passive subpopulation, inferred from longitudinal surveys. The recoverability threshold t_P^{\max} is defined as the duration at which the required activation signal to achieve P_{P \to R} = 0.5 equals \rho_{\max} under baseline conditions. Empirical estimation from unemployment scarring studies suggests t_P^{\max} \approx 3-5 years.

